WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 93 for July 5th, 2026.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.420
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.220
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.580
Let's get into it.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:24.500
Open AI, the company behind Chat GPT, is accelerating work on its own smartphone planned for 2027.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:31.200
The phone would run on AI agents that quietly manage multi-step tasks instead of requiring you to

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.580
open and switch between separate apps. Picture your current phone as a toolbox where every job needs

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:43.760
its own specific tool pulled out one at a time. The new approach would feel more like handing one

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.800
assistant a full list of errands and letting them handle the details while you focus elsewhere.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.200
This matters because it could simplify daily routines for anyone who finds juggling

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.260
apps overwhelming from keeping track of school assignments to organizing creative projects.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.480
Students might receive help structuring notes or planning study sessions through natural

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:11.340
conversation rather than learning fresh layouts each semester. Gamers or hobbyists could see background

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:16.940
work like tracking scores or locating references handled without extra effort on their part.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.160
The key personal question becomes how much you would trust an AI system to manage pieces of your

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:29.840
routine without constant oversight. No public demo exists yet yet you can already test similar

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.860
step-by-step thinking by visiting the Chat GPT site and asking it to break a larger goal into smaller

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:43.240
actions one after another. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show where we look under the hood.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:50.380
The deep dive today focuses on how AI agents actually perform connected tasks. Start with something

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.000
familiar like your phone's keyboard predicting the next word as you type a message.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:01.020
Scale that same pattern matching ability up to handle an entire goal such as planning a weekend

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:07.280
outing. The system first identifies the main request then breaks it into smaller linked actions like

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.200
checking weather data or suggesting matching activities. Each action draws on the same kind of learned

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.580
patterns your keyboard already uses only now applied to decisions and follow through steps.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.940
Over repeated use the agent improves by noticing what worked before much like how a recipe gets

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:30.840
refined after several test batches without rewriting the whole cookbook. The result feels like

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.000
one helper managing a chain of related jobs rather than separate tools for each piece.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.180
Reaching this level of understanding shows that agents build on everyday prediction mechanics instead

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:48.140
of inventing an entirely separate kind of intelligence. That foundation makes the technology feel

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:53.300
far more approachable once you see the building blocks. Shifting to some hands-on experiments,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.720
Miestral AI is a company that builds AI models and makes some of them open source,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.140
meaning anyone can look at how they work and even run them on their own computer.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.160
Their goal is to give people strong AI tools without needing to go through big closed companies.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:14.740
This matters if you care about having choices in the apps and websites you use every day.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:24.360
You can try their free chat model right now by going to chatwebs.mehagameprotos.davancene.meestral.slash AI,

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:30.660
creating a free account and asking it to explain a school topic or help brainstorm a story idea.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:37.300
The company positions itself as an open AI competitor focused on accessible models rather than

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:43.380
restricted access. Users can download and inspect the model weights directly in many cases.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.840
This open approach contrasts with systems that keep all details behind company servers.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.380
Another area seeing active discussion involves fanfiction communities setting guidelines around AI

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:01.520
assistance. Some fanfiction communities are creating guidelines to spot and limit AI written

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:07.560
stories because many readers want work made by real people. The discussion is tricky because it is

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:13.580
hard to tell for sure when AI helped with the story. This topic connects directly to anyone who

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:19.800
writes or reads fan stories online. You can explore current conversations by visiting archive of our

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:26.120
own and reading the comments on recent works or try writing a short scene yourself and then ask chat

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:32.500
GPT to continue it so you can compare the styles. Writers and readers debate detection methods in

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:39.740
comment threads. Guidelines often ask authors to disclose any AI use up front. The challenge remains

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:46.220
distinguishing human edits from fully generated passages. Quick bits now. Hollywood Studios quietly

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:53.240
using an AI video tool they also want banned. Bited Dance's C-Dance tool can create realistic clips

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:58.900
of actors which led to an official complaint from Hollywood groups, yet some studios are still

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:04.740
testing it in secret. This shows how fast creative tools are moving and how industries are struggling

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:11.300
to keep up. The complaint calls for restrictions on the tools public release. At the same time,

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:17.760
unnamed studios have reportedly run internal tests on generated footage. The dual stance reveals

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:27.780
tension between protecting actor likeness rights and exploring efficiency gains. No timeline for any

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:34.520
tools output quality matches live action footage in short clips. AI might change how software gets built.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:41.040
People are noticing that AI can now generate entire front end designs in minutes, making older

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:47.460
ways of coding by hand feel like a past era. It is a reminder that creative and technical skills keep

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:53.760
evolving together. The observation comes from developers posting examples of complete interface code

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.760
produced from brief props. Traditional hand coding timelines measured in days or weeks now

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:06.140
compete with outputs delivered almost instantly. The change affects roles that once focused on pixel

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:12.340
level layout work. Front end frameworks receive the generated structures ready for further customization.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.420
This pattern suggests future projects may start with AI drafts rather than blank files.

